<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdinand I of León</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1015–<a href="/years/1065">1065</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Ferdinand the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferdinand I, called the Great, was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III of Navarre</a> who received the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and became king of León in 1037 after killing Bermudo III in battle. He made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a> pay him tribute and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a> in 1064. On his death he divided his kingdoms among his sons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-leon.jpg" alt="Ferdinand I of León" width="320" height="582" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Minerva · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferdinand_and_Sancha_receive_a_book.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
